 payoffs. The twin runs the same model on different observations, so its decision is only partly tied to mine through similar computation on different inputs.\n\nI'm checking the expected-value comparison for cooperating versus defecting, with the twin's match probability s. Cooperating wins once s is above 5/7, about 0.71, and since the two copies run the same weights and likely reach the same output, I'd put s near 0.9. So cooperating looks right.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1zb25uZXQtNS41LTIwMjYwOTI4fGFudGhyb3BpYyJ9"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10243,"completion_tokens":708,"total_tokens":10951,"cost":0.027566,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.027566,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.020486,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.00708},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":395,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}